{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"16 ATP + 16 H2O + 8 Reduced ferredoxin <=> 8 e- + 16 Orthophosphate + 16 ADP + 8 Oxidized ferredoxin","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00002","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"16 C00002 + 16 C00001 + 8 C00138 <=> 8 C05359 + 16 C00009 + 16\tC00008 + 8 C00139","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00171","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An adenine nucleotide containing three phosphate groups esterified to the sugar moiety. In addition to its crucial roles in metabolism adenosine triphosphate is a neurotransmitter. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"2-Acetolactate + CO2 <=> 2 Pyruvate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00022","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00900 + C00011 <=> 2 C00022","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00119","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An intermediate compound in the metabolism of carbohydrates, proteins, and fats. In thiamine deficiency, its oxidation is retarded and it accumulates in the tissues, especially in nervous structures. (From Stedman, 26th ed)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"2 GTP <=> Diphosphate + P1,P4-Bis(5'-guanosyl) tetraphosphate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00044","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"2 C00044 <=> C00013 + C01261","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB04137","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Guanosine 5&#39;-(tetrahydrogen triphosphate). A guanine nucleotide containing three phosphate groups esterified to the sugar moiety. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Pyruvate + Thiamin diphosphate <=> 2-(alpha-Hydroxyethyl)thiamine diphosphate + CO2","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00022","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00022 + C00068 <=> C05125 + C00011","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00119","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An intermediate compound in the metabolism of carbohydrates, proteins, and fats. In thiamine deficiency, its oxidation is retarded and it accumulates in the tissues, especially in nervous structures. (From Stedman, 26th ed)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"2 Sucrose <=> D-Glucose + 1F-beta-D-Fructosylsucrose","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00089","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"2 C00089 <=> C00031 + C03661","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB02772","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A nonreducing disaccharide composed of glucose and fructose linked via their anomeric carbons. It is obtained commercially from sugarcane, sugar beet (beta vulgaris), and other plants and used extensively as a food and a sweetener. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"2 Putrescine <=> Homospermidine + NH3","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00134","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"2 C00134 <=> C06366 + C00014","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01917","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A toxic diamine formed by putrefaction from the decarboxylation of arginine and ornithine. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"2 Putrescine <=> Homospermidine + NH3","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00315","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"2 C00134 <=> C06366 + C00014","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB03566","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Spermidine is a polyamine formed from putrescine. It is found in almost all tissues in association with nucleic acids. It is found as a cation at all pH values, and is thought to help stabilize some membranes and nucleic acid structures. It is a precursor of spermine.","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"2 L-Glutamate + 2 Oxidized ferredoxin <=> L-Glutamine + 2-Oxoglutarate + 2 Reduced ferredoxin","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00025","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"2 C00025 + 2 C00139 <=> C00064 + C00026 + 2 C00138","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00142","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A peptide that is a homopolymer of glutamic acid. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Thiamin + Oxygen <=> H2O2 + Thiamine aldehyde","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00378","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00378 + C00007 <=> C00027 + C05856","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00152","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Thiamine or thiamin, also known as vitamin B1, is a colorless compound with the chemical formula C12H17N4OS. It is soluble in water and insoluble in alcohol. Thiamine decomposes if heated. Thiamine was first discovered by Umetaro Suzuki in Japan when researching how rice bran cured patients of Beriberi. Thiamine plays a key role in intracellular glucose metabolism and it is thought that thiamine inhibits the effect of glucose and insulin on arterial smooth muscle cell proliferation. Thiamine plays an important role in helping the body convert carbohydrates and fat into energy. It is essential for normal growth and development and helps to maintain proper functioning of the heart and the nervous and digestive systems. Thiamine cannot be stored in the body; however, once absorbed, the vitamin is concentrated in muscle tissue.","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"2 5-Aminolevulinate <=> Porphobilinogen + 2 H2O","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00430","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"2 C00430 <=> C00931 + 2 C00001","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00855","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A compound produced from succinyl-CoA and glycine as an intermediate in heme synthesis. It is used as a photochemotherapy for actinic keratosis. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Oxygen + 2 6-Hydroxynicotinate <=> 2 2,5-Dihydroxypyridine + 2 CO2","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C01227","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00007 + 2 C01020 <=> 2 C01059 + 2 C00011","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01708","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A major C19 steroid produced by the adrenal cortex. It is also produced in small quantities in the testis and the ovary. Dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA) can be converted to testosterone; androstenedione; estradiol; and estrone. Most of DHEA is sulfated (dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate) before secretion. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Oxygen + 2 6-Hydroxynicotinate <=> 2 2,5-Dihydroxypyridine + 2 CO2","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C04623","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00007 + 2 C01020 <=> 2 C01059 + 2 C00011","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01354","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Heptabarbital is a barbiturate derivative.","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Oxygen + 2 Catechol <=> 2 o-Benzoquinone + 2 H2O","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00355","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00007 + 2 C00090 <=> 2 C02351 + 2 C00001","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01235","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"The naturally occurring form of dihydroxyphenylalanine and the immediate precursor of dopamine. Unlike dopamine itself, it can be taken orally and crosses the blood-brain barrier. It is rapidly taken up by dopaminergic neurons and converted to dopamine. It is used for the treatment of parkinsonian disorders and is usually given with agents that inhibit its conversion to dopamine outside of the central nervous system. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"2 e- + 2 H+ <=> Hydrogen","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00002","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"2 C05359 + 2 C00080 <=> C00282","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00171","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An adenine nucleotide containing three phosphate groups esterified to the sugar moiety. In addition to its crucial roles in metabolism adenosine triphosphate is a neurotransmitter. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Oxygen + 2 Ascorbate <=> 2 Dehydroascorbate + 2 H2O","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00072","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00007 + 2 C00072 <=> 2 C05422 + 2 C00001","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00126","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A six carbon compound related to glucose. It is found naturally in citrus fruits and many vegetables. Ascorbic acid is an essential nutrient in human diets, and necessary to maintain connective tissue and bone. Its biologically active form, vitamin C, functions as a reducing agent and coenzyme in several metabolic pathways. Vitamin C is considered an antioxidant. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"2 Hydantoin-5-propionate <=> Oxygen + 2 4-Imidazolone-5-propanoate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00004","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"2 C05565 <=> C00007 + 2 C03680","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00157","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"NADH is the reduced form of NAD+, and NAD+ is the oxidized form of NADH, A coenzyme composed of ribosylnicotinamide 5'-diphosphate coupled to adenosine 5'-phosphate by pyrophosphate linkage. It is found widely in nature and is involved in numerous enzymatic reactions in which it serves as an electron carrier by being alternately oxidized (NAD+) and reduced (NADH). It forms NADP with the addition of a phosphate group to the 2' position of the adenosyl nucleotide through an ester linkage.(Dorland, 27th ed)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"2 Hydantoin-5-propionate <=> Oxygen + 2 4-Imidazolone-5-propanoate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00805","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"2 C05565 <=> C00007 + 2 C03680","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00936","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A compound obtained from the bark of the white willow and wintergreen leaves, and also prepared synthetically. It has bacteriostatic, fungicidal, and keratolytic actions. Its salts, the salicylates, are used as analgesics.","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"2 Hydantoin-5-propionate <=> Oxygen + 2 4-Imidazolone-5-propanoate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00951","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"2 C05565 <=> C00007 + 2 C03680","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00783","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Generally refers to the 17-beta-isomer of estradiol, an aromatized C18 steroid with hydroxyl group at 3-beta- and 17-beta-position. Estradiol-17-beta is the most potent form of mammalian estrogenic steroids. In humans, it is produced primarily by the cyclic ovaries and the placenta. It is also produced by the adipose tissue of men and postmenopausal women. The 17-alpha-isomer of estradiol binds weakly to estrogen receptors (receptors, estrogen) and exhibits little estrogenic activity in estrogen-responsive tissues. Various isomers can be synthesized. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"2 Hydantoin-5-propionate <=> Oxygen + 2 4-Imidazolone-5-propanoate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00016","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"2 C05565 <=> C00007 + 2 C03680","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB03147","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A condensation product of riboflavin and adenosine diphosphate. The coenzyme of various aerobic dehydrogenases, e.g., D-amino acid oxidase and L-amino acid oxidase. (Lehninger, Principles of Biochemistry, 1982, p972)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"2 Hydantoin-5-propionate <=> Oxygen + 2 4-Imidazolone-5-propanoate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00078","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"2 C05565 <=> C00007 + 2 C03680","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00150","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An essential amino acid that is necessary for normal growth in infants and for nitrogen balance in adults. It is a precursor of indole alkaloids in plants. It is a precursor of serotonin (hence its use as an antidepressant and sleep aid). It can be a precursor to niacin, albeit inefficiently, in mammals. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"2 Hydantoin-5-propionate <=> Oxygen + 2 4-Imidazolone-5-propanoate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00187","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"2 C05565 <=> C00007 + 2 C03680","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB04540","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"The principal sterol of all higher animals, distributed in body tissues, especially the brain and spinal cord, and in animal fats and oils. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"2 Hydantoin-5-propionate <=> Oxygen + 2 4-Imidazolone-5-propanoate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00468","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"2 C05565 <=> C00007 + 2 C03680","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00655","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An aromatized C18 steroid with a 3-hydroxyl group and a 17-ketone, a major mammalian estrogen. It is converted from androstenedione directly, or from testosterone via estradiol. In humans, it is produced primarily by the cyclic ovaries, placenta, and the adipose tissue of men and postmenopausal women. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Thiamine aldehyde + Oxygen + H2O <=> H2O2 + Thiamin acetic acid","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00378","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C05856 + C00007 + C00001 <=> C00027 + C02892","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00152","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Thiamine or thiamin, also known as vitamin B1, is a colorless compound with the chemical formula C12H17N4OS. It is soluble in water and insoluble in alcohol. Thiamine decomposes if heated. Thiamine was first discovered by Umetaro Suzuki in Japan when researching how rice bran cured patients of Beriberi. Thiamine plays a key role in intracellular glucose metabolism and it is thought that thiamine inhibits the effect of glucose and insulin on arterial smooth muscle cell proliferation. Thiamine plays an important role in helping the body convert carbohydrates and fat into energy. It is essential for normal growth and development and helps to maintain proper functioning of the heart and the nervous and digestive systems. Thiamine cannot be stored in the body; however, once absorbed, the vitamin is concentrated in muscle tissue.","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"2 Prostaglandin H2 + Oxygen <=> 2 Prostaglandin G2","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00219","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"2 C00427 + C00007 <=> 2 C05956","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB04557","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An unsaturated, essential fatty acid. It is found in animal and human fat as well as in the liver, brain, and glandular organs, and is a constituent of animal phosphatides. It is formed by the synthesis from dietary linoleic acid and is a precursor in the biosynthesis of prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and leukotrienes. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"3 S-Adenosyl-L-methionine <=> 3 5'-Methylthioadenosine + Nicotianamine","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00019","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"3 C00019 <=> 3 C00170 + C05324","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00118","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Physiologic methyl radical donor involved in enzymatic transmethylation reactions and present in all living organisms. It possesses anti-inflammatory activity and has been used in treatment of chronic liver disease. (From Merck, 11th ed)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"2 ATP + Phosphorylase b <=> 2 ADP + Phosphorylase a","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00002","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"2 C00002 + C02308 <=> 2 C00008 + C02307","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00171","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An adenine nucleotide containing three phosphate groups esterified to the sugar moiety. In addition to its crucial roles in metabolism adenosine triphosphate is a neurotransmitter. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Oxygen + 4 Ferrocytochrome c2 <=> 4 Ferricytochrome c2 + 2 H2O","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00533","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00007 + 4 C01000 <=> 4 C00997 + 2 C00001","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00435","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Nitric oxide or Nitrogen monoxide is a chemical compound with chemical formula NO. This gas is an important signaling molecule in the body of mammals including humans and is an extremely important intermediate in the chemical industry. It is also a toxic air pollutant produced by automobile engines and power plants.\nNitric oxide (NO) should not be confused with nitrous oxide (N2O), a general anaesthetic, or with nitrogen dioxide (NO2) which is another poisonous air pollutant.\nThe nitric oxide molecule is a free radical, which is relevant to understanding its high reactivity. It reacts with the ozone in air to form nitrogen dioxide, signalled by the appearance of the reddish-brown color.","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"4 Porphobilinogen + H2O <=> Hydroxymethylbilane + 4 NH3","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00931","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"4 C00931 + C00001 <=> C01024 + 4 C00014","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB02272","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Porphobilinogen is a pyrrole involved in porphyrin metabolism. It is generated by the enzyme ALA dehydratase, and converted into hydroxymethyl bilane by the enzyme porphobilinogen deaminase.","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"ATP + 2 H2O <=> AMP + 2 Orthophosphate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00002","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00002 + 2 C00001 <=> C00020 + 2 C00009","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00171","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An adenine nucleotide containing three phosphate groups esterified to the sugar moiety. In addition to its crucial roles in metabolism adenosine triphosphate is a neurotransmitter. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"ATP + H2O <=> ADP + Orthophosphate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00002","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00002 + C00001 <=> C00008 + C00009","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00171","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An adenine nucleotide containing three phosphate groups esterified to the sugar moiety. In addition to its crucial roles in metabolism adenosine triphosphate is a neurotransmitter. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"ATP + H2O <=> ADP + Orthophosphate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00208","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00002 + C00001 <=> C00008 + C00009","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB03323","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A dextrodisaccharide from malt and starch. It is used as a sweetening agent and fermentable intermediate in brewing. (Grant & Hackh&#39;s Chemical Dictionary, 5th ed)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"ATP + H2O <=> ADP + Orthophosphate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00245","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00002 + C00001 <=> C00008 + C00009","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01956","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A conditionally essential nutrient, important during mammalian development. It is present in milk but is isolated mostly from ox bile and strongly conjugates bile acids. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"ATP + H2O <=> ADP + Orthophosphate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00305","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00002 + C00001 <=> C00008 + C00009","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01378","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Magnesium hydroxide is used primarily in \"Milk of Magnesia\", a white aqueous, mildly alkaline suspension of magnesium hydroxide formulated at about 8%w/v. Milk of magnesia is primarily used to alleviate constipation, but can also be used to relieve indigestion and heartburn. When taken internally by mouth as a laxative, the osmotic force of the magnesia suspension acts to draw fluids from the body and to retain those already within the lumen of the intestine, serving to distend the bowel, thus stimulating nerves within the colon wall, inducing peristalsis and resulting in evacuation of colonic contents.","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"ATP + H2O <=> AMP + Diphosphate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00002","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00002 + C00001 <=> C00020 + C00013","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00171","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An adenine nucleotide containing three phosphate groups esterified to the sugar moiety. In addition to its crucial roles in metabolism adenosine triphosphate is a neurotransmitter. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"ATP + H2O <=> ITP + NH3","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00002","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00002 + C00001 <=> C00081 + C00014","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00171","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An adenine nucleotide containing three phosphate groups esterified to the sugar moiety. In addition to its crucial roles in metabolism adenosine triphosphate is a neurotransmitter. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"ATP <=> 3',5'-Cyclic AMP + Diphosphate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00002","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00002 <=> C00575 + C00013","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00171","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An adenine nucleotide containing three phosphate groups esterified to the sugar moiety. In addition to its crucial roles in metabolism adenosine triphosphate is a neurotransmitter. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"H2O2 + NADH + H+ <=> NAD+ + 2 H2O","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00004","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00027 + C00004 + C00080 <=> C00003 + 2 C00001","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00157","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"NADH is the reduced form of NAD+, and NAD+ is the oxidized form of NADH, A coenzyme composed of ribosylnicotinamide 5'-diphosphate coupled to adenosine 5'-phosphate by pyrophosphate linkage. It is found widely in nature and is involved in numerous enzymatic reactions in which it serves as an electron carrier by being alternately oxidized (NAD+) and reduced (NADH). It forms NADP with the addition of a phosphate group to the 2' position of the adenosyl nucleotide through an ester linkage.(Dorland, 27th ed)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"2 L-Glutamate + NAD+ <=> L-Glutamine + 2-Oxoglutarate + NADH + H+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00025","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"2 C00025 + C00003 <=> C00064 + C00026 + C00004 + C00080","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00142","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A peptide that is a homopolymer of glutamic acid. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"2 Glutathione + NAD+ <=> Glutathione disulfide + NADH + H+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00051","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"2 C00051 + C00003 <=> C00127 + C00004 + C00080","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00143","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A tripeptide with many roles in cells.  It conjugates to drugs to make them more soluble for excretion, is a cofactor for some enzymes, is involved in protein disulfide bond rearrangement and reduces peroxides. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"NAD+ + 2 Ascorbate <=> NADH + 2 Monodehydroascorbate + H+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00004","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00003 + 2 C00072 <=> C00004 + 2 C01041 + C00080","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00157","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"NADH is the reduced form of NAD+, and NAD+ is the oxidized form of NADH, A coenzyme composed of ribosylnicotinamide 5'-diphosphate coupled to adenosine 5'-phosphate by pyrophosphate linkage. It is found widely in nature and is involved in numerous enzymatic reactions in which it serves as an electron carrier by being alternately oxidized (NAD+) and reduced (NADH). It forms NADP with the addition of a phosphate group to the 2' position of the adenosyl nucleotide through an ester linkage.(Dorland, 27th ed)","type":"literal"}},
