{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"alpha-Amino acid + H2O + NAD+ <=> 2-Oxo acid + NH3 + NADH + H+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00065","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C05167 + C00001 + C00003 <=> C00161 + C00014 + C00004 + C00080","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00133","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A non-essential amino acid occurring in natural form as the L-isomer. It is synthesized from glycine or threonine. It is involved in the biosynthesis of purines; pyrimidines; and other amino acids. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"alpha-Amino acid + H2O + NAD+ <=> 2-Oxo acid + NH3 + NADH + H+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00183","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C05167 + C00001 + C00003 <=> C00161 + C00014 + C00004 + C00080","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00161","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A branched-chain essential amino acid that has stimulant activity. It promotes muscle growth and tissue repair. It is a precursor in the penicillin biosynthetic pathway. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"alpha-Amino acid + H2O + NAD+ <=> 2-Oxo acid + NH3 + NADH + H+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00047","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C05167 + C00001 + C00003 <=> C00161 + C00014 + C00004 + C00080","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00123","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"L-Lysine (abbreviated as Lys or K)[1] is an ��-amino acid with the chemical formula HO2CCH(NH2)(CH2)4NH2. This amino acid is an essential amino acid, which means that humans cannot synthesize it. Its codons are AAA and AAG.\nL-Lysine is a base, as are arginine and histidine. The ��-amino group often participates in hydrogen bonding and as a general base in catalysis. Common posttranslational modifications include methylation of the ��-amino group, giving methyl-, dimethyl-, and trimethyllysine. The latter occurs in calmodulin. Other posttranslational modifications include acetylation. Collagen contains hydroxylysine which is derived from lysine by lysyl hydroxylase. O-Glycosylation of lysine residues in the endoplasmic reticulum or Golgi apparatus is used to mark certain proteins for secretion from the cell.","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"alpha-Amino acid + H2O + NAD+ <=> 2-Oxo acid + NH3 + NADH + H+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00025","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C05167 + C00001 + C00003 <=> C00161 + C00014 + C00004 + C00080","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00142","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A peptide that is a homopolymer of glutamic acid. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"alpha-Amino acid + H2O + NADP+ <=> 2-Oxo acid + NH3 + NADPH + H+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00065","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C05167 + C00001 + C00006 <=> C00161 + C00014 + C00005 + C00080","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00133","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A non-essential amino acid occurring in natural form as the L-isomer. It is synthesized from glycine or threonine. It is involved in the biosynthesis of purines; pyrimidines; and other amino acids. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"alpha-Amino acid + H2O + NADP+ <=> 2-Oxo acid + NH3 + NADPH + H+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00183","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C05167 + C00001 + C00006 <=> C00161 + C00014 + C00005 + C00080","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00161","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A branched-chain essential amino acid that has stimulant activity. It promotes muscle growth and tissue repair. It is a precursor in the penicillin biosynthetic pathway. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"alpha-Amino acid + H2O + NADP+ <=> 2-Oxo acid + NH3 + NADPH + H+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00047","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C05167 + C00001 + C00006 <=> C00161 + C00014 + C00005 + C00080","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00123","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"L-Lysine (abbreviated as Lys or K)[1] is an ��-amino acid with the chemical formula HO2CCH(NH2)(CH2)4NH2. This amino acid is an essential amino acid, which means that humans cannot synthesize it. Its codons are AAA and AAG.\nL-Lysine is a base, as are arginine and histidine. The ��-amino group often participates in hydrogen bonding and as a general base in catalysis. Common posttranslational modifications include methylation of the ��-amino group, giving methyl-, dimethyl-, and trimethyllysine. The latter occurs in calmodulin. Other posttranslational modifications include acetylation. Collagen contains hydroxylysine which is derived from lysine by lysyl hydroxylase. O-Glycosylation of lysine residues in the endoplasmic reticulum or Golgi apparatus is used to mark certain proteins for secretion from the cell.","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"alpha-Amino acid + H2O + NADP+ <=> 2-Oxo acid + NH3 + NADPH + H+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00025","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C05167 + C00001 + C00006 <=> C00161 + C00014 + C00005 + C00080","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00142","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A peptide that is a homopolymer of glutamic acid. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"NH3 + Oxygen + 2 H+ <=> Hydroxylamine + H2O","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00004","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00014 + C00007 + 2 C00080 <=> C00192 + C00001","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00157","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"NADH is the reduced form of NAD+, and NAD+ is the oxidized form of NADH, A coenzyme composed of ribosylnicotinamide 5'-diphosphate coupled to adenosine 5'-phosphate by pyrophosphate linkage. It is found widely in nature and is involved in numerous enzymatic reactions in which it serves as an electron carrier by being alternately oxidized (NAD+) and reduced (NADH). It forms NADP with the addition of a phosphate group to the 2' position of the adenosyl nucleotide through an ester linkage.(Dorland, 27th ed)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"NH3 + Oxygen + 2 H+ <=> Hydroxylamine + H2O","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C07130","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00014 + C00007 + 2 C00080 <=> C00192 + C00001","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00277","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A methyl xanthine derivative from tea with diuretic, smooth muscle relaxant, bronchial dilation, cardiac and central nervous system stimulant activities.  Theophylline inhibits the 3&#39;,5&#39;-cyclic nucleotide phosphodiesterase that degrades cyclic AMP thus potentiates the actions of agents that act through adenylate cyclase and cyclic AMP. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"NH3 + Oxygen + 2 H+ <=> Hydroxylamine + H2O","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C07480","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00014 + C00007 + 2 C00080 <=> C00192 + C00001","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01412","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"3,7-Dimethylxanthine. The principle alkaloid in Theobroma cacao (the cacao bean) and other plants. A xanthine alkaloid that is used as a bronchodilator and as a vasodilator. It has a weaker diuretic activity than theophylline and is also a less powerful stimulant of smooth muscle. It has practically no stimulant effect on the central nervous system. It was formerly used as a diuretic and in the treatment of angina pectoris and hypertension. (From Martindale, The Extra Pharmacopoeia, 30th ed, pp1318-9)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"NH3 + Oxygen + 2 H+ <=> Hydroxylamine + H2O","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C07481","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00014 + C00007 + 2 C00080 <=> C00192 + C00001","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00201","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A methylxanthine naturally occurring in some beverages and also used as a pharmacological agent. Caffeine&#39;s most notable pharmacological effect is as a central nervous system stimulant, increasing alertness and producing agitation. It also relaxes smooth muscle, stimulates cardiac muscle, stimulates diuresis, and appears to be useful in the treatment of some types of headache. Several cellular actions of caffeine have been observed, but it is not entirely clear how each contributes to its pharmacological profile. Among the most important are inhibition of cyclic nucleotide phosphodiesterases, antagonism of adenosine receptors, and modulation of intracellular calcium handling. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
