{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Formaldehyde + NAD+ + H2O <=> Formate + NADH + H+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00067","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00067 + C00003 + C00001 <=> C00058 + C00004 + C00080","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB03843","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A highly reactive aldehyde gas formed by oxidation or incomplete combustion of hydrocarbons. In solution, it has a wide range of uses: in the manufacture of resins and textiles, as a disinfectant, and as a laboratory fixative or preservative. Formaldehyde solution (formalin) is considered a hazardous compound, and its vapor toxic. (From Reynolds, Martindale The Extra Pharmacopoeia, 30th ed, p717)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"2 Formaldehyde + H2O <=> Formate + Methanol","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00067","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"2 C00067 + C00001 <=> C00058 + C00132","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB03843","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A highly reactive aldehyde gas formed by oxidation or incomplete combustion of hydrocarbons. In solution, it has a wide range of uses: in the manufacture of resins and textiles, as a disinfectant, and as a laboratory fixative or preservative. Formaldehyde solution (formalin) is considered a hazardous compound, and its vapor toxic. (From Reynolds, Martindale The Extra Pharmacopoeia, 30th ed, p717)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"ATP + Thiamin diphosphate <=> ADP + Thiamin triphosphate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00002","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00002 + C00068 <=> C00008 + C03028","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00171","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An adenine nucleotide containing three phosphate groups esterified to the sugar moiety. In addition to its crucial roles in metabolism adenosine triphosphate is a neurotransmitter. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"ATP + Thiamin diphosphate <=> ADP + Thiamin triphosphate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00068","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00002 + C00068 <=> C00008 + C03028","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01987","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"The coenzyme form of Vitamin B1 present in many animal tissues. It is a required intermediate in the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex and the ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complex. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"ATP + Thiamin diphosphate <=> ADP + Thiamin triphosphate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00378","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00002 + C00068 <=> C00008 + C03028","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00152","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Thiamine or thiamin, also known as vitamin B1, is a colorless compound with the chemical formula C12H17N4OS. It is soluble in water and insoluble in alcohol. Thiamine decomposes if heated. Thiamine was first discovered by Umetaro Suzuki in Japan when researching how rice bran cured patients of Beriberi. Thiamine plays a key role in intracellular glucose metabolism and it is thought that thiamine inhibits the effect of glucose and insulin on arterial smooth muscle cell proliferation. Thiamine plays an important role in helping the body convert carbohydrates and fat into energy. It is essential for normal growth and development and helps to maintain proper functioning of the heart and the nervous and digestive systems. Thiamine cannot be stored in the body; however, once absorbed, the vitamin is concentrated in muscle tissue.","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"ATP + Thiamin monophosphate <=> ADP + Thiamin diphosphate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00002","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00002 + C01081 <=> C00008 + C00068","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00171","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An adenine nucleotide containing three phosphate groups esterified to the sugar moiety. In addition to its crucial roles in metabolism adenosine triphosphate is a neurotransmitter. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"ATP + Thiamin monophosphate <=> ADP + Thiamin diphosphate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C01081","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00002 + C01081 <=> C00008 + C00068","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB03416","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Thiamine dihydrogen phosphate ester. The monophosphate ester of thiamine. Synonyms: monophosphothiamine; vitamin B1 monophosphate. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"ATP + Thiamin <=> AMP + Thiamin diphosphate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00002","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00002 + C00378 <=> C00020 + C00068","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00171","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An adenine nucleotide containing three phosphate groups esterified to the sugar moiety. In addition to its crucial roles in metabolism adenosine triphosphate is a neurotransmitter. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"ATP + Thiamin <=> AMP + Thiamin diphosphate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00378","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00002 + C00378 <=> C00020 + C00068","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00152","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Thiamine or thiamin, also known as vitamin B1, is a colorless compound with the chemical formula C12H17N4OS. It is soluble in water and insoluble in alcohol. Thiamine decomposes if heated. Thiamine was first discovered by Umetaro Suzuki in Japan when researching how rice bran cured patients of Beriberi. Thiamine plays a key role in intracellular glucose metabolism and it is thought that thiamine inhibits the effect of glucose and insulin on arterial smooth muscle cell proliferation. Thiamine plays an important role in helping the body convert carbohydrates and fat into energy. It is essential for normal growth and development and helps to maintain proper functioning of the heart and the nervous and digestive systems. Thiamine cannot be stored in the body; however, once absorbed, the vitamin is concentrated in muscle tissue.","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Primary alcohol + Acceptor <=> Aldehyde + Reduced acceptor","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00314","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00226 + C00028 <=> C00071 + C00030","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00165","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"The 4-methanol form of vitamin B 6 which is converted to pyridoxal phosphate which is a coenzyme for synthesis of amino acids, neurotransmitters (serotonin, norepinephrine), sphingolipids, aminolevulinic acid.  Although pyridoxine and Vitamin B 6 are still frequently used as synonyms, especially by medical researchers, this practice is erroneous and sometimes misleading (EE Snell; Ann NY Acad Sci, vol 585 pg 1, 1990). [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"L-Galactono-1,4-lactone + 2 Ferricytochrome c <=> Ascorbate + 2 Ferrocytochrome c + 2 H+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00072","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C01115 + 2 C00125 <=> C00072 + 2 C00126 + 2 C00080","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00126","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A six carbon compound related to glucose. It is found naturally in citrus fruits and many vegetables. Ascorbic acid is an essential nutrient in human diets, and necessary to maintain connective tissue and bone. Its biologically active form, vitamin C, functions as a reducing agent and coenzyme in several metabolic pathways. Vitamin C is considered an antioxidant. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Ascorbate + H2O2 <=> Dehydroascorbate + 2 H2O","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00072","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00072 + C00027 <=> C05422 + 2 C00001","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00126","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A six carbon compound related to glucose. It is found naturally in citrus fruits and many vegetables. Ascorbic acid is an essential nutrient in human diets, and necessary to maintain connective tissue and bone. Its biologically active form, vitamin C, functions as a reducing agent and coenzyme in several metabolic pathways. Vitamin C is considered an antioxidant. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Ascorbate <=> Monodehydroascorbate + H+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00072","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00072 <=> C01041 + C00080","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00126","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A six carbon compound related to glucose. It is found naturally in citrus fruits and many vegetables. Ascorbic acid is an essential nutrient in human diets, and necessary to maintain connective tissue and bone. Its biologically active form, vitamin C, functions as a reducing agent and coenzyme in several metabolic pathways. Vitamin C is considered an antioxidant. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Ascorbate <=> Monodehydroascorbate + H+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C03582","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00072 <=> C01041 + C00080","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB02709","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Resveratrol (3,5,4'-trihydroxystilbene) is a polyphenolic phytoalexin. It is a stilbenoid, a derivate of stilbene, and is produced in plants with the help of the enzyme stilbene synthase. It exists as two structural isomers: cis-(Z) and trans-(E), with the trans-isomer shown in the top image. The trans- form can undergo isomerisation to the cis- form when heated or exposed to ultraviolet irradiation. In a 2004 issue of Science, Dr. Sinclair of Harvard University said resveratrol is not an easy molecule to protect from oxidation. It has been claimed that it is readily degraded by exposure to light, heat, and oxygen. However, studies find that Trans-resveratrol undergoes negligible oxidation in normal atmosphere at room temperature. [Wikipedia]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Ascorbate + Oxygen + H2O <=> Threonate + Oxalate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00072","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00072 + C00007 + C00001 <=> C01620 + C00209","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00126","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A six carbon compound related to glucose. It is found naturally in citrus fruits and many vegetables. Ascorbic acid is an essential nutrient in human diets, and necessary to maintain connective tissue and bone. Its biologically active form, vitamin C, functions as a reducing agent and coenzyme in several metabolic pathways. Vitamin C is considered an antioxidant. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"S-Adenosyl-L-methionine + L-Methionine <=> S-Adenosyl-L-homocysteine + S-Methyl-L-methionine","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00019","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00019 + C00073 <=> C00021 + C03172","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00118","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Physiologic methyl radical donor involved in enzymatic transmethylation reactions and present in all living organisms. It possesses anti-inflammatory activity and has been used in treatment of chronic liver disease. (From Merck, 11th ed)","type":"literal"}},
