{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"L-Valine + NADP+ + H2O <=> 3-Methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid + NH3 + NADPH + H+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00183","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00183 + C00006 + C00001 <=> C00141 + C00014 + C00005 + C00080","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00161","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A branched-chain essential amino acid that has stimulant activity. It promotes muscle growth and tissue repair. It is a precursor in the penicillin biosynthetic pathway. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"L-Valine + Pyruvate <=> 3-Methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid + L-Alanine","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00022","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00183 + C00022 <=> C00141 + C00041","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00119","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An intermediate compound in the metabolism of carbohydrates, proteins, and fats. In thiamine deficiency, its oxidation is retarded and it accumulates in the tissues, especially in nervous structures. (From Stedman, 26th ed)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"L-Valine + Pyruvate <=> 3-Methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid + L-Alanine","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00183","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00183 + C00022 <=> C00141 + C00041","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00161","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A branched-chain essential amino acid that has stimulant activity. It promotes muscle growth and tissue repair. It is a precursor in the penicillin biosynthetic pathway. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"5,10-Methylenetetrahydrofolate + Reduced ferredoxin <=> 5-Methyltetrahydrofolate + Oxidized ferredoxin","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00440","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00143 + C00138 <=> C00440 + C00139","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB04789","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"5-Methyltetrahydrofolate is a methylated derivate of tetrahydrofolate. It is generated by methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase from 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate and used to recycle homocysteine back to methionine by 5-methyltetrahydrofolate-homocysteine methyltransferases (also called methionine synthases).","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Glycine + Tetrahydrofolate + NAD+ <=> 5,10-Methylenetetrahydrofolate + NH3 + CO2 + NADH + H+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00101","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00037 + C00101 + C00003 <=> C00143 + C00014 + C00011 + C00004 +\tC00080","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00116","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Tetrahydrofolic acid is a folic acid derivative. It is produced from dihydrofolic acid by dihydrofolate reductase.\nIt is converted into 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate by serine hydroxymethyltransferase. It is a coenzyme in many reactions, especially in the metabolism of amino acids and nucleic acids. It acts as a donor of a group with one carbon atom. It gets this carbon atom by sequestering formaldehyde produced in other processes.","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"5-Methyltetrahydrofolate + NADP+ <=> 5,10-Methylenetetrahydrofolate + NADPH + H+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00440","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00440 + C00006 <=> C00143 + C00005 + C00080","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB04789","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"5-Methyltetrahydrofolate is a methylated derivate of tetrahydrofolate. It is generated by methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase from 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate and used to recycle homocysteine back to methionine by 5-methyltetrahydrofolate-homocysteine methyltransferases (also called methionine synthases).","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"ATP + Guanosine <=> ADP + GMP","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00002","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00002 + C00387 <=> C00008 + C00144","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00171","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An adenine nucleotide containing three phosphate groups esterified to the sugar moiety. In addition to its crucial roles in metabolism adenosine triphosphate is a neurotransmitter. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
