{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Homocystine + 2 Glutathione <=> 2 L-Homocysteine + Glutathione disulfide","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00051","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C01817 + 2 C00051 <=> 2 C00155 + C00127","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00143","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A tripeptide with many roles in cells.  It conjugates to drugs to make them more soluble for excretion, is a cofactor for some enzymes, is involved in protein disulfide bond rearrangement and reduces peroxides. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Benzoate + Oxygen + NADPH + H+ <=> 4-Hydroxybenzoate + NADP+ + H2O","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00180","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00180 + C00007 + C00005 + C00080 <=> C00156 + C00006 + C00001","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB03793","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A fungistatic compound that is widely used as a food preservative. It is conjugated to GLYCINE in the liver and excreted as hippuric acid. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"4-Hydroxybenzoate + Oxygen + NADH + H+ <=> 3,4-Dihydroxybenzoate + NAD+ + H2O","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00004","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00156 + C00007 + C00004 + C00080 <=> C00230 + C00003 + C00001","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00157","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"NADH is the reduced form of NAD+, and NAD+ is the oxidized form of NADH, A coenzyme composed of ribosylnicotinamide 5'-diphosphate coupled to adenosine 5'-phosphate by pyrophosphate linkage. It is found widely in nature and is involved in numerous enzymatic reactions in which it serves as an electron carrier by being alternately oxidized (NAD+) and reduced (NADH). It forms NADP with the addition of a phosphate group to the 2' position of the adenosyl nucleotide through an ester linkage.(Dorland, 27th ed)","type":"literal"}},
