{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"L-Glutamine + Phenylpyruvate <=> 2-Oxoglutaramate + L-Phenylalanine","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00064","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00064 + C00166 <=> C00940 + C00079","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00130","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A non-essential amino acid present abundantly throughout the body and is involved in many metabolic processes. It is synthesized from glutamic acid and ammonia. It is the principal carrier of nitrogen in the body and is an important energy source for many cells. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"L-Tryptophan + Phenylpyruvate <=> Indolepyruvate + L-Phenylalanine","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00078","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00078 + C00166 <=> C00331 + C00079","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00150","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An essential amino acid that is necessary for normal growth in infants and for nitrogen balance in adults. It is a precursor of indole alkaloids in plants. It is a precursor of serotonin (hence its use as an antidepressant and sleep aid). It can be a precursor to niacin, albeit inefficiently, in mammals. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"UTP + D-Glucuronate 1-phosphate <=> Diphosphate + UDP-glucuronate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00075","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00075 + C05385 <=> C00013 + C00167","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB04005","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Uridine 5&#39;-(tetrahydrogen triphosphate). A uracil nucleotide containing three phosphate groups esterified to the sugar moiety. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"ATP + Carbamate <=> ADP + Carbamoyl phosphate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00002","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00002 + C01563 <=> C00008 + C00169","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00171","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An adenine nucleotide containing three phosphate groups esterified to the sugar moiety. In addition to its crucial roles in metabolism adenosine triphosphate is a neurotransmitter. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Carbamoyl phosphate + L-Lysine <=> Orthophosphate + L-Homocitrulline","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00047","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00169 + C00047 <=> C00009 + C02427","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00123","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"L-Lysine (abbreviated as Lys or K)[1] is an ��-amino acid with the chemical formula HO2CCH(NH2)(CH2)4NH2. This amino acid is an essential amino acid, which means that humans cannot synthesize it. Its codons are AAA and AAG.\nL-Lysine is a base, as are arginine and histidine. The ��-amino group often participates in hydrogen bonding and as a general base in catalysis. Common posttranslational modifications include methylation of the ��-amino group, giving methyl-, dimethyl-, and trimethyllysine. The latter occurs in calmodulin. Other posttranslational modifications include acetylation. Collagen contains hydroxylysine which is derived from lysine by lysyl hydroxylase. O-Glycosylation of lysine residues in the endoplasmic reticulum or Golgi apparatus is used to mark certain proteins for secretion from the cell.","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Carbamoyl phosphate + L-Aspartate <=> Orthophosphate + N-Carbamoyl-L-aspartate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00049","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00169 + C00049 <=> C00009 + C00438","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00128","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"One of the non-essential amino acids commonly occurring in the L-form. It is found in animals and plants, especially in sugar cane and sugar beets. It may be a neurotransmitter. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Carbamoyl phosphate + Putrescine <=> Orthophosphate + N-Carbamoylputrescine","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00134","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00169 + C00134 <=> C00009 + C00436","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01917","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A toxic diamine formed by putrefaction from the decarboxylation of arginine and ornithine. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"5'-Methylthioadenosine + H2O <=> Adenine + 5-Methylthio-D-ribose","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00021","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00170 + C00001 <=> C00147 + C03089","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01752","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"5&#39;-S-(3-Amino-3-carboxypropyl)-5&#39;-thioadenosine. Formed from S-adenosylmethionine after transmethylation reactions. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
