{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"L-Valine <=> 2-Methylpropanamine + CO2","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00183","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00183 <=> C02787 + C00011","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00161","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A branched-chain essential amino acid that has stimulant activity. It promotes muscle growth and tissue repair. It is a precursor in the penicillin biosynthetic pathway. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"D-Xylulose 5-phosphate + Formaldehyde <=> D-Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate + Glycerone","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00067","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00231 + C00067 <=> C00118 + C00184","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB03843","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A highly reactive aldehyde gas formed by oxidation or incomplete combustion of hydrocarbons. In solution, it has a wide range of uses: in the manufacture of resins and textiles, as a disinfectant, and as a laboratory fixative or preservative. Formaldehyde solution (formalin) is considered a hazardous compound, and its vapor toxic. (From Reynolds, Martindale The Extra Pharmacopoeia, 30th ed, p717)","type":"literal"}},
