{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"3,4-Dihydroxybenzoate + Oxygen + NADPH + H+ <=> Benzene-1,2,4-triol + CO2 + NADP+ + H2O","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00004","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00230 + C00007 + C00005 + C00080 <=> C02814 + C00011 + C00006 +\tC00001","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00157","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"NADH is the reduced form of NAD+, and NAD+ is the oxidized form of NADH, A coenzyme composed of ribosylnicotinamide 5'-diphosphate coupled to adenosine 5'-phosphate by pyrophosphate linkage. It is found widely in nature and is involved in numerous enzymatic reactions in which it serves as an electron carrier by being alternately oxidized (NAD+) and reduced (NADH). It forms NADP with the addition of a phosphate group to the 2' position of the adenosyl nucleotide through an ester linkage.(Dorland, 27th ed)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"3,4-Dihydroxybenzoate + Oxygen + NADPH + H+ <=> Benzene-1,2,4-triol + CO2 + NADP+ + H2O","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00219","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00230 + C00007 + C00005 + C00080 <=> C02814 + C00011 + C00006 +\tC00001","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB04557","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An unsaturated, essential fatty acid. It is found in animal and human fat as well as in the liver, brain, and glandular organs, and is a constituent of animal phosphatides. It is formed by the synthesis from dietary linoleic acid and is a precursor in the biosynthesis of prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and leukotrienes. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"4-Sulfobenzoate + Oxygen + NADH + H+ <=> 3,4-Dihydroxybenzoate + Sulfite + NAD+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00004","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C02236 + C00007 + C00004 + C00080 <=> C00230 + C00094 + C00003","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00157","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"NADH is the reduced form of NAD+, and NAD+ is the oxidized form of NADH, A coenzyme composed of ribosylnicotinamide 5'-diphosphate coupled to adenosine 5'-phosphate by pyrophosphate linkage. It is found widely in nature and is involved in numerous enzymatic reactions in which it serves as an electron carrier by being alternately oxidized (NAD+) and reduced (NADH). It forms NADP with the addition of a phosphate group to the 2' position of the adenosyl nucleotide through an ester linkage.(Dorland, 27th ed)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"ATP + D-Xylulose <=> ADP + D-Xylulose 5-phosphate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00002","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00002 + C00310 <=> C00008 + C00231","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00171","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An adenine nucleotide containing three phosphate groups esterified to the sugar moiety. In addition to its crucial roles in metabolism adenosine triphosphate is a neurotransmitter. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"4-Hydroxybutanoic acid + NAD+ <=> Succinate semialdehyde + NADH + H+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00989","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00989 + C00003 <=> C00232 + C00004 + C00080","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01440","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Gamma Hydroxybutyric Acid, commonly abbreviated GHB, is a therapeutic drug which is illegal in multiple countries. It is currently regulated in the US and sold by Jazz Pharmaceuticals under the name Xyrem. However, it is important to note that GHB is a designated Orphan drug (in 1985). Today Xyrem is a Schedule III drug;\nhowever GHB remains a Schedule I drug and the illicit use of Xyrem falls under penalties of Schedule I. GHB is a naturally occurring substance found in the central nervous system, wine, beef, small citrus fruits and almost all other living creatures in small amounts. It is used illegally under the street names Juice, Liquid Ecstasy or simply G, either as an intoxicant, or as a date rape drug. Xyrem is a central nervous system depressant that reduces excessive daytime sleepiness and cataplexy in patients with narcolepsy.","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"4-Hydroxy-2-oxo-heptanedioate <=> Succinate semialdehyde + Pyruvate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00022","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C05601 <=> C00232 + C00022","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00119","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An intermediate compound in the metabolism of carbohydrates, proteins, and fats. In thiamine deficiency, its oxidation is retarded and it accumulates in the tissues, especially in nervous structures. (From Stedman, 26th ed)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"4-Hydroxy-2-oxo-heptanedioate <=> Succinate semialdehyde + Pyruvate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00037","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C05601 <=> C00232 + C00022","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00145","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A non-essential amino acid. It is found primarily in gelatin and silk fibroin and used therapeutically as a nutrient. It is also a fast inhibitory neurotransmitter. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"4-Hydroxy-2-oxo-heptanedioate <=> Succinate semialdehyde + Pyruvate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00067","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C05601 <=> C00232 + C00022","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB03843","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A highly reactive aldehyde gas formed by oxidation or incomplete combustion of hydrocarbons. In solution, it has a wide range of uses: in the manufacture of resins and textiles, as a disinfectant, and as a laboratory fixative or preservative. Formaldehyde solution (formalin) is considered a hazardous compound, and its vapor toxic. (From Reynolds, Martindale The Extra Pharmacopoeia, 30th ed, p717)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Succinate semialdehyde + Pyruvate <=> 2,4-Dihydroxyhept-2-enedioate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00022","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00232 + C00022 <=> C06201","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00119","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An intermediate compound in the metabolism of carbohydrates, proteins, and fats. In thiamine deficiency, its oxidation is retarded and it accumulates in the tissues, especially in nervous structures. (From Stedman, 26th ed)","type":"literal"}},
