{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"S-Adenosyl-L-methionine + Carnosine <=> S-Adenosyl-L-homocysteine + beta-Alanyl-N(pi)-methyl-L-histidine","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00019","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00019 + C00386 <=> C00021 + C01262","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00118","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Physiologic methyl radical donor involved in enzymatic transmethylation reactions and present in all living organisms. It possesses anti-inflammatory activity and has been used in treatment of chronic liver disease. (From Merck, 11th ed)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Guanosine + H2O <=> Xanthosine + NH3","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00387","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00387 + C00001 <=> C01762 + C00014","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB02857","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Guanosine is a nucleoside comprising guanine attached to a ribose (ribofuranose) ring via a ��-N9-glycosidic bond. Guanosine can be phosphorylated to become GMP (guanosine monophosphate), cGMP (cyclic guanosine monophosphate), GDP (guanosine diphosphate) and GTP (guanosine triphosphate). [Wikipedia]","type":"literal"}},
