{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"1H-Imidazole-4-ethanamine + Acetyl-CoA <=> 4-(beta-Acetylaminoethyl)imidazole + CoA","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00037","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00388 + C00024 <=> C05135 + C00010","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00145","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A non-essential amino acid. It is found primarily in gelatin and silk fibroin and used therapeutically as a nutrient. It is also a fast inhibitory neurotransmitter. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"1H-Imidazole-4-ethanamine + Acetyl-CoA <=> 4-(beta-Acetylaminoethyl)imidazole + CoA","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00047","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00388 + C00024 <=> C05135 + C00010","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00123","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"L-Lysine (abbreviated as Lys or K)[1] is an ��-amino acid with the chemical formula HO2CCH(NH2)(CH2)4NH2. This amino acid is an essential amino acid, which means that humans cannot synthesize it. Its codons are AAA and AAG.\nL-Lysine is a base, as are arginine and histidine. The ��-amino group often participates in hydrogen bonding and as a general base in catalysis. Common posttranslational modifications include methylation of the ��-amino group, giving methyl-, dimethyl-, and trimethyllysine. The latter occurs in calmodulin. Other posttranslational modifications include acetylation. Collagen contains hydroxylysine which is derived from lysine by lysyl hydroxylase. O-Glycosylation of lysine residues in the endoplasmic reticulum or Golgi apparatus is used to mark certain proteins for secretion from the cell.","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"1H-Imidazole-4-ethanamine + Acetyl-CoA <=> 4-(beta-Acetylaminoethyl)imidazole + CoA","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00083","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00388 + C00024 <=> C05135 + C00010","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB04524","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A coenzyme A derivative which plays a key role in the fatty acid synthesis in the cytoplasmic and microsomal systems. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"S-Adenosyl-L-methionine + 1H-Imidazole-4-ethanamine <=> S-Adenosyl-L-homocysteine + N-Methylhistamine","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00019","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00019 + C00388 <=> C00021 + C05127","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00118","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Physiologic methyl radical donor involved in enzymatic transmethylation reactions and present in all living organisms. It possesses anti-inflammatory activity and has been used in treatment of chronic liver disease. (From Merck, 11th ed)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Quercetin + Oxygen <=> 2-Protocatechoylphloroglucinolcarboxylate + CO","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00389","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00389 + C00007 <=> C04524 + C00237","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB04216","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A flavonol widely distributed in plants. It is an antioxidant, like many other phenolic heterocyclic compounds. Glycosylated forms include RUTIN and quercetrin. [PubChem]","type":"literal"}},
