{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Ubiquinol + NAD+ <=> Ubiquinone + NADH + H+","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00004","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00390 + C00003 <=> C00399 + C00004 + C00080","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00157","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"NADH is the reduced form of NAD+, and NAD+ is the oxidized form of NADH, A coenzyme composed of ribosylnicotinamide 5'-diphosphate coupled to adenosine 5'-phosphate by pyrophosphate linkage. It is found widely in nature and is involved in numerous enzymatic reactions in which it serves as an electron carrier by being alternately oxidized (NAD+) and reduced (NADH). It forms NADP with the addition of a phosphate group to the 2' position of the adenosyl nucleotide through an ester linkage.(Dorland, 27th ed)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Ubiquinone + Succinate <=> Ubiquinol + Fumarate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00042","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00399 + C00042 <=> C00390 + C00122","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00139","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"A water-soluble, colorless crystal with an acid taste that is used as a chemical intermediate, in medicine, the manufacture of lacquers, and to make perfume esters. It is also used in foods as a sequestrant, buffer, and a neutralizing agent. (Hawley&#39;s Condensed Chemical Dictionary, 12th ed, p1099; McGraw-Hill Dictionary of Scientific and Technical Terms, 4th ed, p1851)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"Ubiquinol + Acceptor <=> Ubiquinone + Reduced acceptor","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00004","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00390 + C00028 <=> C00399 + C00030","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00157","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"NADH is the reduced form of NAD+, and NAD+ is the oxidized form of NADH, A coenzyme composed of ribosylnicotinamide 5'-diphosphate coupled to adenosine 5'-phosphate by pyrophosphate linkage. It is found widely in nature and is involved in numerous enzymatic reactions in which it serves as an electron carrier by being alternately oxidized (NAD+) and reduced (NADH). It forms NADP with the addition of a phosphate group to the 2' position of the adenosyl nucleotide through an ester linkage.(Dorland, 27th ed)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"GDP-glucose + D-Glucose 6-phosphate <=> GDP + alpha,alpha'-Trehalose 6-phosphate","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00092","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00394 + C00092 <=> C00035 + C00689","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB03581","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"An ester of glucose with phosphoric acid, made in the course of glucose metabolism by mammalian and other cells. It is a normal constituent of resting muscle and probably is in constant equilibrium with fructose-6-phosphate. (Stedman, 26th ed)","type":"literal"}},
{"ReactionTitle":{"value":"S-Adenosyl-L-methionine + Tryptamine <=> S-Adenosyl-L-homocysteine + N-Methyltryptamine","type":"literal"},"compound":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cpd:C00019","type":"uri"},"ChemicalEquation":{"value":"C00019 + C00398 <=> C00021 + C06213","type":"literal"},"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00118","type":"uri"},"drugDesc":{"value":"Physiologic methyl radical donor involved in enzymatic transmethylation reactions and present in all living organisms. It possesses anti-inflammatory activity and has been used in treatment of chronic liver disease. (From Merck, 11th ed)","type":"literal"}},
